A destroy that does not revoke below the pool's own node
leaves capabilities naming dead objects,
and they come back to life when the memory is rebuilt into the same type.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: dangling object capability

diff --git a/kernel/pool.c b/kernel/pool.c
--- a/kernel/pool.c
+++ b/kernel/pool.c
@@ -85,12 +85,6 @@ bool pool_destroy(struct pool *pool, struct cap *keep, bool preempt)
             }
         }
     }
-    while (cap_revoke_step_except(&pool->node, keep)) {
-        LOOP_PAID(pool_destroy, node, "a capability to the pool or to one of its objects");
-        if (cap_stop_here(preempt)) {
-            return false;
-        }
-    }
 
     /*
      * The objects go newest first, and the used mark follows,
